<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pazyryk burials</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pazyryk kurgans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pazyryk burials are a group of kurgans of the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE in the Pazyryk valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> mountains in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, excavated in 1929 and, by Sergei Rudenko, in 1947–1949. Ice formed in the tombs preserved tattooed bodies, felt hangings, saddles, textiles and the oldest known knotted pile carpet, now in the Hermitage Museum. They give their name to the Pazyryk culture of the Scythian-Saka world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pazyryk-burials.jpg" alt="Pazyryk burials" width="320" height="292" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mercedes González-Ruiz,Cristina Santos,Xavier Jordana,Marc Simón,Carles Lalueza-Fox,Elena Gigli,Maria Pilar Aluja,Assumpció Malgosa … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pazyryk_burial_from_Baga_Turgen_Gol_site,_Bayan-%C3%96lgiy_province,_Western_Mongolia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Pazyryk</b> tombs in the Altai, linked to the wider Saka-Scythian world, filled with ice soon after burial and so preserved the oldest known knotted pile carpet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
